\title{Cellular Automata as Algebraic Varieties over the Binary Field}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 9, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Every elementary cellular automaton (ECA) can be presented as a polynomial map of
degree at most three over the binary field $\GF$. Under this presentation the
configurations left invariant by a rule---its \emph{fixed points}, or still
lifes---form the $\GF$-points of an affine variety $V(g)$ cut out by $n$ cubic
equations on a cyclic lattice of length $n$. We develop this dictionary rigorously
and use it to test a natural conjecture: that the dimension of $V(g)$ measures the
dynamical complexity of the rule in the sense of Wolfram's classification. We prove
the conjecture \emph{false}, and in the strongest possible way. The computationally
universal Rule~110 has a zero-dimensional fixed-point variety (a single point),
while the dynamically trivial identity Rule~204 has the maximal dimension $n$; on
every cyclic lattice of length at least two the identity's variety has exponentially
more points than Rule~110's. We accompany the refutation with an exact
classification of the additive rules, whose varieties are linear subspaces with
dimensions governed by elementary arithmetic---for Rule~90 by the Pisano period
$\pi(2)=3$ realized as the multiplicative order of the Fibonacci companion matrix
over $\GF$, and for Rule~150 by parity. The corrected principle that emerges is
that the honest algebraic invariant separating tame from universal rules is the
\emph{degree} of the defining polynomial---equivalently, the \emph{linearity} of
the variety---and not its dimension.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Elementary cellular automata are among the simplest dynamical systems capable of
complex behavior. A one-dimensional cyclic array of binary cells evolves in
discrete time; each cell's next value depends only on its own current value and
those of its two nearest neighbors. There are $2^{2^3}=256$ such local rules,
catalogued by Wolfram as Rules $0$ through $255$ and grouped empirically into four
complexity classes: (1)~evolution to a homogeneous state, (2)~evolution to periodic
or nested structures, (3)~chaotic behavior, and (4)~localized structures with
long-range interactions. Rule~110 belongs to Class~4 and is Turing-complete.

We study these systems through the lens of algebraic geometry over the field of two
elements. The starting point is elementary but consequential: because every Boolean
function of three variables coincides with a unique multilinear polynomial over
$\GF$, each ECA rule is a polynomial map of degree at most three, and its set of
fixed configurations is an affine variety. This paper makes that correspondence
precise, computes the fixed-point varieties of the landmark rules exactly, and
evaluates the conjecture that variety dimension tracks complexity class.

Our contributions are: (1)~a rigorous formulation of the automaton--variety
dictionary; (2)~exact determination of the fixed-point varieties of Rules
$0,51,170,204,240,90,150,110$; (3)~a transfer-matrix bridge relating Rule~90 to the
Fibonacci companion matrix over $\GF$ and to the Pisano period; and (4)~the
refutation of the complexity--dimension conjecture and the corrected principle that
degree/linearity, not dimension, is the relevant invariant.

\section{Preliminaries: the binary field}

Throughout, the alphabet is the binary field $\GF=\{0,1\}$ with addition modulo $2$
(so $1+1=0$) and ordinary multiplication. Two identities are used repeatedly:
$2x=0$ for all $x$, and $x^2=x$ for $x\in\{0,1\}$. The latter implies that any
polynomial function $\{0,1\}^k\to\{0,1\}$ equals a unique \emph{multilinear}
polynomial; its degree is at most $k$.

\section{The automaton--variety dictionary}

\begin{definition}[Configuration]
Fix a cycle length $n\ge 1$. A \emph{configuration} is a function
$s:\Zn\to\GF$; equivalently a vector $s=(s_0,\dots,s_{n-1})\in\GF^n$ with indices
read modulo $n$. The set of configurations is $\Config(n)=\GF^n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Local rule and global step]
A \emph{local rule} is a function $g:\GF^3\to\GF$, presented as its multilinear
polynomial. The induced \emph{global step} is
\[
\step_g:\Config(n)\to\Config(n),\qquad (\step_g\,s)_i=g(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1}),
\]
with all indices modulo $n$, so that the lattice is a cycle.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fixed-point variety]
A configuration $s$ is \emph{fixed} by $g$ if $\step_g\,s=s$. The \emph{fixed-point
variety} is
\[
V(g)=\{\,s\in\Config(n):\step_g\,s=s\,\},
\]
the $\GF$-solution set of the $n$ polynomial equations
$s_i=g(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1})$. Since each $g$ has degree $\le 3$, $V(g)$ is an affine
variety cut out by equations of degree $\le 3$.
\end{definition}

For a variety that is a linear (or affine) subspace of dimension $d$, we have
$|V(g)|=2^d$, so counting $\GF$-points determines the dimension.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c l l c}
\hline
Rule & Behavior & Polynomial $g(a,b,c)$ & Degree \\
\hline
$0$   & null        & $0$                   & $0$ \\
$204$ & identity    & $b$                   & $1$ \\
$51$  & complement  & $b+1$                 & $1$ (affine) \\
$170$ & left shift  & $c$                   & $1$ \\
$240$ & right shift & $a$                   & $1$ \\
$90$  & additive    & $a+c$                 & $1$ \\
$150$ & additive    & $a+b+c$               & $1$ \\
$110$ & universal   & $b+c+bc+abc$          & $3$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{lemma}[Cyclic propagation]\label{lem:prop}
Let $P$ be a predicate on $\Zn$ (with $n\ge 1$). If $P(i)\Rightarrow P(i+1)$ for all
$i$, and $P(i_0)$ holds for some $i_0$, then $P(j)$ holds for all $j$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By induction, $P(i_0+k)$ holds for every natural number $k$. Every $j$ can be
written $j=i_0+m$ for a suitable representative $m$, so $P(j)$ holds.
\end{proof}

\section{The extreme rules: null, identity, complement}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 0, single point]
For every $n$, $V(\mathrm{rule}_0)=\{0\}$; the variety is a single point of
dimension $0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The fixed-point equation is $s_i=0$ for every $i$, so $s=0$ is the only solution.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 204, whole space]\label{thm:204}
For every $n$, every configuration is fixed: $V(\mathrm{rule}_{204})=\GF^n$, of
dimension $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The equation is $s_i=s_i$, satisfied identically.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 51, empty variety]
For $n\ge 1$ the complement rule has no fixed configuration:
$V(\mathrm{rule}_{51})=\varnothing$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The equation at site $0$ reads $s_0=s_0+1$, i.e. $0=1$ in $\GF$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{The shift rules: the constant line}

\begin{theorem}[Rules 170 and 240, dimension 1]
For $n\ge 1$, each shift rule fixes exactly the constant configurations; the variety
is a line of dimension $1$ (two points).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For Rule~170 the fixed-point equation is $s_i=s_{i+1}$ for all $i$; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:prop} applied to $P(i):s_i=s_0$, every cell equals $s_0$, so $s$ is
constant. Conversely every constant configuration is fixed. Rule~240
($s_i=s_{i-1}$) is identical after reindexing.
\end{proof}

\section{The additive rules: linearity and arithmetic}

The defining feature of Rules~90 and~150 is that their global steps are
$\GF$-\emph{linear} maps, so their fixed-point sets are kernels of linear
operators---genuine linear subspaces.

\begin{theorem}[Rule 90 is a linear variety, Fibonacci form]\label{thm:90}
The step of Rule~90 is the linear map $s\mapsto(i\mapsto s_{i-1}+s_{i+1})$, and
$V(\mathrm{rule}_{90})=\ker(\step_{90}-\id)$ is a linear subspace. A configuration
is fixed iff it satisfies the Fibonacci recurrence over $\GF$,
\[
s_{i+1}=s_i+s_{i-1}\qquad(i\in\Zn).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Linearity of $s\mapsto s_{i-1}+s_{i+1}$ is immediate. The fixed-point equation
$s_i=s_{i-1}+s_{i+1}$ rearranges, using $2=0$, to $s_{i+1}=s_i+s_{i-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 150 is a linear variety, two-periodic form]
The step of Rule~150 is the linear map $s\mapsto(i\mapsto s_{i-1}+s_i+s_{i+1})$, and
its fixed points are exactly the two-periodic configurations $s_{i+2}=s_i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The fixed-point equation $s_i=s_{i-1}+s_i+s_{i+1}$ gives $s_{i-1}+s_{i+1}=0$, i.e.
$s_{i+1}=s_{i-1}$; reindexing yields $s_{i+2}=s_i$.
\end{proof}

These characterizations translate directly into dimensions. Two-periodicity
decouples the even and odd sublattices when $n$ is even (dimension $2$) and forces
global constancy when $n$ is odd (dimension $1$). The Fibonacci case is governed by
number theory.

\subsection{The Fibonacci companion matrix and the Pisano period}

Rule~90's recurrence $s_{i+1}=s_i+s_{i-1}$ is advanced by the \emph{Fibonacci
companion matrix} over $\GF$,
\[
T=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}\in M_2(\GF).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Order of $T$]\label{thm:T}
$T^3=I$ and $T\ne I$, so the multiplicative order of $T$ is $3$. Consequently, for
every $n$, $T^n=I\iff 3\mid n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct computation gives $T^3=I$; since $T\ne I$ and $3$ is prime, the order is
exactly $3$. The divisibility statement is $T^n=I\iff\ord(T)\mid n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transfer-matrix bridge]
If $s$ is a Rule~90 fixed configuration, then for every $i$,
\[
\begin{pmatrix}s_{i+1}\\s_{i+2}\end{pmatrix}
=T\begin{pmatrix}s_i\\s_{i+1}\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Thus the fixed points of Rule~90 are exactly the closed orbits of $T$, and a
solution closes up on a cycle of length $n$ iff $T^n=I$, i.e. iff $3\mid n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The bottom row is the recurrence $s_{i+2}=s_{i+1}+s_i$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:90};
the top row is $s_{i+1}=s_{i+1}$. Iterating $n$ times around the cycle returns the
initial state pair for all initial pairs iff $T^n=I$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pisano period, sequence form]
The Fibonacci numbers are periodic modulo $2$ with period $3$: $F_{n+3}\equiv F_n
\pmod 2$, giving $0,1,1,0,1,1,\dots$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From $F_{n+3}=F_n+2F_{n+1}$ and $2\equiv 0\pmod 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Rule 90 dimension]
The fixed-point variety of Rule~90 has dimension $2$ (four points) when $3\mid n$
and dimension $0$ otherwise. Small-cycle counts confirm the pattern: $|V|=4$ for
$n=3,6$ and $|V|=1$ for $n=4,5$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The universal rule and the refutation of the conjecture}

Rule~110 is the genuinely nonlinear case: its polynomial $g(a,b,c)=b+c+bc+abc$
carries a degree-$3$ monomial, and its step is not a linear map.

\begin{lemma}[Zero propagation]\label{lem:zero}
If $s$ is fixed by Rule~110 and $s_i=0$, then $s_{i+1}=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Substituting $b=s_i=0$ into the fixed-point equation
$s_i=s_i+s_{i+1}+s_is_{i+1}+s_{i-1}s_is_{i+1}$ kills every term with a factor of
$s_i$, leaving $0=s_{i+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[All-ones is not fixed]\label{lem:ones}
The all-ones configuration is not fixed by Rule~110.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Evaluating the rule on $(1,1,1)$ gives $1+1+1+1=0\ne 1$, so site $0$ fails to be
fixed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 110 collapse]\label{thm:110}
For every $n\ge 1$, $V(\mathrm{rule}_{110})=\{0\}$: the Turing-complete rule fixes
only the zero configuration, a zero-dimensional variety.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $s$ be fixed. If some cell is $0$, Lemma~\ref{lem:zero} and cyclic propagation
(Lemma~\ref{lem:prop}) force every cell to be $0$. Otherwise every cell is $1$,
i.e.\ $s$ is all-ones---impossible by Lemma~\ref{lem:ones}. Hence $s=0$; and $s=0$
is manifestly fixed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity--dimension conjecture, refuted]
On every cyclic lattice of length $n\ge 1$, the identity Rule~204 (Class~2) fixes
the entire space $\GF^n$, whereas the computationally universal Rule~110 (Class~4)
fixes only the zero configuration. Therefore fixed-point dimension does not increase
with dynamical complexity.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative inversion]
For every $n\ge 2$,
$|V(\mathrm{rule}_{110})|=1<2^n=|V(\mathrm{rule}_{204})|$, and the gap grows
exponentially in $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$|V(\mathrm{rule}_{204})|=2^n$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:204};
$|V(\mathrm{rule}_{110})|=1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:110}; and $2^n\ge 2^2>1$ for
$n\ge 2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion: degree, not dimension}

The refutation is sharp and its direction is instructive. Not only does dimension
fail to increase with complexity---the ordering is \emph{inverted}: the most complex
rule attains the minimal dimension and the most trivial the maximal. Any monotone
``complexity $=$ dimension'' law is therefore untenable.

The pattern that does hold organizes the rules by the \emph{degree} of their
defining polynomials. The rules with large, structured varieties---Rules
$90,150,170,240,204$---are exactly the affine ones (degree $\le 1$). Their
fixed-point sets are linear subspaces; their dimensions are computed by finite-field
linear algebra and elementary number theory (Pisano periods, parities, orders of
companion matrices); and they populate Wolfram's tame Classes~1--2. Rule~110 is a
genuine cubic, and it is precisely its nonlinearity---the $abc$ term---that both
collapses its fixed-point variety and underlies its universal computational power.

We therefore propose the corrected principle: the algebraic invariant that
distinguishes tame from universal ECAs is the \emph{degree} of the local
polynomial---equivalently, whether the fixed-point variety is \emph{linear}---and
not the dimension of that variety. Complexity is encoded in the curvature of the
geometry, not its size.

\section{Future work}

\textbf{(1) Linearity as the true separator.} We conjecture that among the $256$
rules, exactly the sixteen affine rules have fixed-point sets that are affine
subspaces for every cycle length, and every rule of genuine degree $\ge 2$ admits a
cycle length on which its fixed-point set is not closed under addition of solutions.

\textbf{(2) Fixed-point dimension as a Pisano-type function.} For each additive rule
the map $n\mapsto\dim V_n$ should be eventually periodic, with period equal to the
multiplicative order over $\GF$ of the companion matrix of the rule's recurrence:
order $3$ (the Pisano period $\pi(2)$) for Rule~90, and in general the order of $x$
in $\GF[x]/(p)$ for recurrence characteristic polynomial $p$.

\textbf{(3) A period spectrum for the additive rules.} The sixteen affine rules
should split into families indexed by the $\GF$-factorization type of their
recurrence polynomials, with two additive rules conjugate under lattice symmetries
iff their recurrence polynomials share order and degree pattern.

\section{Conclusion}

Elementary cellular automata are affine varieties over the binary field, and their
still lifes are the $\GF$-points of those varieties. The natural conjecture that
variety dimension measures dynamical complexity is false and in fact inverted: the
universal Rule~110 collapses to a single point while the trivial identity fills the
whole space. The additive rules admit exact linear descriptions with arithmetically
controlled dimensions. The invariant that genuinely separates the tame from the
universal is the degree of the defining polynomial---the linearity or nonlinearity
of the fixed-point variety---a qualitative feature invisible to any head-count of
solutions.

